.Dd November 20, 2026
.Dt BACKLIGHT 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm backlight
.Nd show and set the PicoCalc backlights
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Ar level
.Nm
.Cm lcd | kbd
.Op Ar level
.Nm
.Fl s
.Op Cm lcd
.Op Ar level
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
reads and sets the two lights the PicoCalc's keyboard controller drives:
.Cm lcd ,
the screen, and
.Cm kbd ,
the keys.
Without arguments it prints both levels, one light per line, as
.Dq lcd 160
and
.Dq kbd 0 .
A light name alone prints that light's level.
A
.Ar level
sets the named light, or the screen when no light is named, and prints
the level the controller kept.
.Pp
A level is a decimal number from 0 to 240.
The controller rounds what it is given: the screen level goes down to a
multiple of 16 and never below 16, so the screen stays lit, and the
keyboard level goes down to a multiple of 32, with anything below 32
turning the keys' light off.
.Pp
The keyboard's own shortcut keys change the same levels, and
.Nm
reports whatever the controller holds at that moment.
.Pp
The options are:
.Bl -tag -width "-s"
.It Fl s
After setting the screen level, or with no level the current one, write
the level the controller kept to
.Pa /etc/backlight ,
the screen level
.Pa /etc/rc
applies at every boot.
Only the superuser may use it, and it applies to the screen only.
.El
.Pp
.Nm
is installed setuid root because the kernel accepts a write to its
.Va machdep.picocalc
nodes from the superuser only; with it, any user can change the lights.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width "/etc/backlight"
.It Pa /etc/backlight
the screen level for boot: one decimal number.
At power-on the controller starts the screen at 32, dim enough to look
black, and
.Pa /etc/rc
replaces that with this level, or with 160 when the file is missing or
its content is not a decimal number of one to three digits.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Nm
exits 0 on success, 1 when the kernel has no PicoCalc keyboard
controller, the controller does not answer,
.Fl s
is refused or
.Pa /etc/backlight
cannot be written, and 2 on an argument it does not accept.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Dim the screen and light the keys:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ backlight 96
lcd 96
$ backlight kbd 64
kbd 64
.Ed
.Pp
Make the current screen level the boot level, as the superuser:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# backlight -s
lcd 96
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr sysctl 8
